{
  "image_name": "1a03d0d2d0823378f8390b7397d2db3c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how cancer-associated fibroblasts (CAFs) can support tumor progression through three connected roles: inflammatory signaling, metabolic support, and extracellular matrix remodeling. In the center, a CAF is shown interacting with surrounding tumor cells and the microenvironment.\n\nOn the left, CAFs release pro-inflammatory cytokines, notably IL-6, IL-8, and IL-1β, along with other cytokines and possibly extracellular vesicles. These signals appear to activate macrophages, promote inflammation, and alter immune cells such as CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, collectively shaping an immune environment that favors tumor growth.\n\nIn the middle, the CAF is depicted as metabolically active, with autophagy inside the cell and arrows indicating transfer of nutrients to tumor cells. Amino acids are specifically highlighted, and possible lipid-related exchange is suggested, implying that CAFs may help feed tumor cells and enhance their proliferation and survival.\n\nOn the right, CAFs contribute to matrix remodeling by degrading procollagen 1 and promoting collagen secretion and ECM deposition. This produces a dense, desmoplastic extracellular matrix with increased stiffness. The remodeled ECM is linked to neoangiogenesis near blood vessels and further supports tumor cell growth. Overall, the image presents CAFs as central organizers of a tumor-promoting microenvironment.",
